{-
   The program copy-to-alias elision exists not to break - Analysis-IR-JS.md §6.3, and §10.2's first
   negative row, "source mutated during a proposed copy alias".

   `both(p, p)` is legal for a trivially copyable `p` **because the shared argument is a copy**: it
   holds no loan, so the `&` argument beside it is an exclusive borrow of storage nothing else has a
   name for. That is the one program in which turning the copy into an alias would be observed - the
   callee's write through `q` would land in the object `p` reads out of, and the first line of the
   callee would answer 99 and 98 rather than 1 and 2.

   What refuses it on the JavaScript target is not a special case at the call. The elision asks its
   read-only walk of the **source** as well as of the copy, and `&p` is both a position the callee
   may write and a `Value` place a store names - so the call is declined from the source's side
   before anything is said about the argument list. Weaken that walk and this fixture answers 30
   under Node while the native build goes on answering 31.

   `sum(moved)` is the same question one level in and is where the rewrite's conservatism is visible
   rather than its safety: the write is *after* the borrow, so a live-range-aware version could alias
   it, and the whole-body walk this slice built refuses it outright.

   # resolver: ownership

   **The shipping stage does not agree with itself about this program**, which is why the directive
   is here rather than the fixture being compiled by both. `-no-ir-opt` through `compiler/resolve`
   answers 1088 where an optimized build of the same program answers 12, on both targets: the
   unoptimized build hands `both` the caller's storage at the shared position and the optimizer is
   what turns it back into a copy. That is a defect in that stage and not in this one - the rewrite
   answers 12 either way - and it is recorded in
   `doc/progress/2026-09-22-J72-jsir-alias-scalarize.md` rather than being worked around here.
-}

data Point {x: Int, y: Int}

-- The shared parameter and the exclusive one name the same storage at the one call below, which is
-- what makes it the fixture: only the copy keeps them apart.
@noinline fn both(p: Point, &q: Point) -> Int:
    q = Point {x: 99, y: 98}
    return p.x * 10 + p.y

@noinline fn sum(p: Point) -> Int = p.x * 10 + p.y

fn main() -> Int:
    let &score = 0 :: Int

    let &p = Point {x: 1, y: 2}

    -- 12: what `both` saw is the copy, made before it wrote through `q`. An alias answers 998.
    if both(p, p) == 12: score = score + 1

    -- And the write did happen, so the copy was the argument rather than the whole call.
    if p.x == 99: score = score + 2
    if p.y == 98: score = score + 4

    let &moved = Point {x: 3, y: 4}
    let held = sum(moved)
    moved.x = 7

    -- 34 before the write, 74 after it.
    if held == 34: score = score + 8
    if moved.x * 10 + moved.y == 74: score = score + 16

    return score
